1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,318
The most famous water heater upgrade is going tankless.

2
00:00:03,618 --> 00:00:09,029
But if your water heater runs on electricity, going tankless saves you almost nothing.

3
00:00:09,329 --> 00:00:12,994
There's a different upgrade that cuts that bill by about seventy percent.

4
00:00:13,294 --> 00:00:14,488
Let's run the numbers.

5
00:00:14,788 --> 00:00:22,898
In this video, I'll compare the three main types of water heaters: a regular tank, a tankless unit, and a heat pump water heater.

6
00:00:23,198 --> 00:00:29,104
I'll do it for a home that heats water with electricity, and for one that uses natural gas,

7
00:00:29,404 --> 00:00:36,570
using the official efficiency ratings and the government's own data on how much hot water homes actually use.

8
00:00:36,870 --> 00:00:39,983
By the end, you'll know which one makes sense for your house.

9
00:00:40,283 --> 00:00:41,667
First, why this matters.

10
00:00:41,967 --> 00:00:52,312
In my ranking of household appliances, the electric water heater came in at number three, at about four hundred ninety-two dollars a year for the homes that have one.

11
00:00:52,612 --> 00:00:58,923
That's every shower, every load of laundry, and every dishwasher cycle, all billed to one machine.

12
00:00:59,223 --> 00:01:09,704
And it's split almost evenly across the country: about fifty-nine million homes heat their water with natural gas, and about fifty-seven million with electricity.

13
00:01:10,004 --> 00:01:15,318
So whichever way your home goes, this is one of the biggest numbers on your energy bill.

14
00:01:15,618 --> 00:01:17,052
Now, how each one works.

15
00:01:17,352 --> 00:01:23,027
A regular tank water heater keeps a big tank of water hot all day, whether you use it or not.

16
00:01:23,327 --> 00:01:25,916
A typical one holds around fifty gallons.

17
00:01:26,216 --> 00:01:28,339
A tankless water heater has no tank.

18
00:01:28,639 --> 00:01:34,804
It heats water only when you open a tap, using a powerful gas burner or electric element.

19
00:01:35,104 --> 00:01:41,612
A heat pump water heater looks like a regular tank, but it works like the heat pump we covered in the heating video.

20
00:01:41,912 --> 00:01:47,393
Instead of making heat directly, it pulls heat from the air around it and moves it into the water.

21
00:01:47,693 --> 00:01:53,377
To compare them fairly, there's one number that matters: the Uniform Energy Factor, or UEF.

22
00:01:53,677 --> 00:01:56,522
It comes from a standard Department of Energy test.

23
00:01:56,822 --> 00:02:02,343
The higher the UEF, the less energy the water heater needs for the same hot water.

24
00:02:02,643 --> 00:02:04,603
So here are the official numbers.

25
00:02:04,903 --> 00:02:12,680
Under current federal rules, a standard fifty-gallon electric tank has to reach a UEF of about point nine two.

26
00:02:12,980 --> 00:02:18,008
In other words, almost all the electricity it uses ends up as heat in the water.

27
00:02:18,308 --> 00:02:22,017
An electric tankless heater has to reach about point nine one.

28
00:02:22,317 --> 00:02:23,788
That's basically the same.

29
00:02:24,088 --> 00:02:25,286
That's the key point.

30
00:02:25,586 --> 00:02:34,165
Electric resistance can't get much better than turning one unit of electricity into one unit of heat, whether there's a tank or not.

31
00:02:34,465 --> 00:02:37,052
A heat pump water heater is in another league.

32
00:02:37,352 --> 00:02:44,478
To earn the ENERGY STAR label, a standard heat pump water heater needs a UEF of at least three point three.

33
00:02:44,778 --> 00:02:49,043
That's about three point six times as efficient as the electric tank.

34
00:02:49,343 --> 00:02:55,047
On the gas side, a minimum fifty-gallon gas tank is rated around point five six.

35
00:02:55,347 --> 00:03:02,805
ENERGY STAR gas tanks need at least point eight one, and ENERGY STAR gas tankless units need at least point nine five.

36
00:03:03,105 --> 00:03:08,022
Now let's put dollars on it, starting with a home that heats water with electricity.

37
00:03:08,322 --> 00:03:16,858
The government's household survey says the average electric water heater uses about two thousand seven hundred kilowatt-hours a year.

38
00:03:17,158 --> 00:03:22,333
At today's average price, that's the four hundred ninety-two dollars from my ranking.

39
00:03:22,633 --> 00:03:29,114
Swap that tank for an electric tankless unit, and you'd spend about four hundred ninety-eight dollars a year.

40
00:03:29,414 --> 00:03:31,214
A few dollars more, not less.

41
00:03:31,514 --> 00:03:38,415
Swap it for an ENERGY STAR heat pump water heater, and it drops to about a hundred thirty-seven dollars a year.

42
00:03:38,715 --> 00:03:42,540
That's around three hundred fifty-five dollars saved every year,

43
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:44,712
and a cut of about seventy percent.

44
00:03:45,012 --> 00:03:47,723
So for an electric home, the answer is clear.

45
00:03:48,023 --> 00:03:50,487
Tankless is not the efficiency upgrade.

46
00:03:50,787 --> 00:03:52,606
The heat pump water heater is.

47
00:03:52,906 --> 00:03:55,599
And the savings grow with your electricity price.

48
00:03:55,899 --> 00:04:03,555
A heat pump water heater saves about one thousand nine hundred fifty kilowatt-hours a year compared with an electric tank.

49
00:04:03,855 --> 00:04:05,699
What that's worth depends on your state.

50
00:04:05,999 --> 00:04:11,561
In Washington, with relatively cheap power, that's about two hundred eighty dollars a year.

51
00:04:11,861 --> 00:04:13,892
In Florida, about three hundred.

52
00:04:14,192 --> 00:04:16,674
In Texas, about three hundred thirteen.

53
00:04:16,974 --> 00:04:19,980
In Illinois, it's around three hundred seventy-five.

54
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:23,703
And in states with expensive electricity, it gets big.

55
00:04:24,003 --> 00:04:30,105
About five hundred seventy dollars a year in New York, and over six hundred thirty in California.

56
00:04:30,405 --> 00:04:35,774
So the more you pay per kilowatt-hour, the faster a heat pump water heater pays you back.

57
00:04:36,074 --> 00:04:38,868
Now a home that heats water with natural gas.

58
00:04:39,168 --> 00:04:45,172
The survey says the average gas water heater burns about eighteen million BTUs a year.

59
00:04:45,472 --> 00:04:52,353
With a minimum-efficiency gas tank at today's gas prices, that's about two hundred sixty-nine dollars a year.

60
00:04:52,653 --> 00:04:57,785
An ENERGY STAR gas tank would bring that down to about a hundred eighty-seven dollars.

61
00:04:58,085 --> 00:05:03,268
A tankless gas heater at the federal minimum lands at the same hundred eighty-seven.

62
00:05:03,568 --> 00:05:09,189
An ENERGY STAR tankless unit gets down to about a hundred fifty-nine dollars a year.

63
00:05:09,489 --> 00:05:18,020
And a heat pump water heater, running on electricity at today's average price, would cost about a hundred sixty-five dollars a year.

64
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:20,060
So in a gas home, it's close.

65
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:31,084
The best tankless unit and a heat pump water heater land within a few dollars of each other, and both save about a hundred dollars a year compared with a minimum gas tank.

66
00:05:31,384 --> 00:05:40,081
The big takeaway for a gas home: the biggest jump comes from leaving the minimum-efficiency tank behind, whichever upgrade you choose.

67
00:05:40,381 --> 00:05:43,449
Before you decide, there are a few practical limits.

68
00:05:43,749 --> 00:05:47,274
A tankless unit has to heat water as fast as you use it.

69
00:05:47,574 --> 00:05:57,869
ENERGY STAR gives typical flow rates: about two and a half gallons per minute for a shower, two point two for a sink, and three point three for a clothes washer.

70
00:05:58,169 --> 00:06:06,894
A certified gas tankless unit only has to deliver at least two point eight gallons per minute at a sixty-seven degree temperature rise.

71
00:06:07,194 --> 00:06:12,716
So a shower and a kitchen sink running at the same time can already ask for more than that.

72
00:06:13,016 --> 00:06:15,678
Bigger households often need a larger unit.

73
00:06:15,978 --> 00:06:18,598
A heat pump water heater has its own limits.

74
00:06:18,898 --> 00:06:31,543
ENERGY STAR says it needs to sit in a space that stays between forty and ninety degrees, with about a thousand cubic feet of air around it, like a basement, a garage or a utility room.

75
00:06:31,843 --> 00:06:40,344
It pulls heat out of that air, so the room around it gets a little cooler, which is a bonus in a hot garage and a drawback in a cold one.

76
00:06:40,644 --> 00:06:47,743
And one more data point: only about six and a half percent of American homes have a tankless water heater at all.

77
00:06:48,043 --> 00:06:49,729
Most homes still have a tank.

78
00:06:50,029 --> 00:06:51,499
So here's how to decide.

79
00:06:51,799 --> 00:07:02,499
If you heat water with electricity and you have a basement, garage or utility room with enough space, a heat pump water heater is the clear winner on running costs.

80
00:07:02,799 --> 00:07:04,959
It cuts the bill by about seventy percent.

81
00:07:05,259 --> 00:07:13,204
If you heat water with electricity and you're thinking about tankless to save energy, the efficiency numbers say it won't do much.

82
00:07:13,504 --> 00:07:18,005
Its real benefits are space and endless hot water, not a lower bill.

83
00:07:18,305 --> 00:07:23,046
If you heat with gas, the first step is getting off a minimum-efficiency tank.

84
00:07:23,346 --> 00:07:30,298
An ENERGY STAR tank, an ENERGY STAR tankless unit, or a heat pump water heater all save real money.

85
00:07:30,598 --> 00:07:34,165
And whatever you have, the hot water itself is the cost.

86
00:07:34,465 --> 00:07:41,934
ENERGY STAR says about ninety percent of the energy it takes to run a clothes washer goes into heating the water.

87
00:07:42,234 --> 00:07:49,590
So washing clothes in cold water and taking shorter showers shrink the bill, no matter which machine heats the water.

88
00:07:49,890 --> 00:07:59,703
To sum it up: for an electric home, a heat pump water heater saves around three hundred fifty dollars a year, and electric tankless saves almost nothing.

89
00:08:00,003 --> 00:08:09,071
For a gas home, the best options cluster around a hundred sixty dollars a year, versus about two hundred seventy for a minimum gas tank.

90
00:08:09,371 --> 00:08:16,900
If you're weighing a heat pump for your whole house too, my heat pump versus gas furnace video does the same math for heating.
